# MTMS web app — environment.
#
#   cp deploy/web.env.example ~/mtms/frontend/web.env
#
# Much shorter than the API's, and the reason is worth knowing: almost nothing about this
# program is configured at RUN time. The two settings that matter most are compiled in by
# `next build` and cannot be changed here.
#
# --- What is NOT here, and why -----------------------------------------------
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL — build time only. The NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix is not a naming
# convention; it is what puts the value into the browser bundle at all. Setting it here does
# nothing. Build with it EMPTY (`NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL= npm run build`, which is what
# scripts/release.sh does) so every call from the browser is relative and this server proxies
# it. That buys four things at once: no domain compiled in, so one build works on localhost, on
# an IP and on a domain later; no CORS, because it is one origin; login over plain HTTP,
# because a same-origin cookie needs no SameSite=None; and one open port instead of two.
#
# API_PROXY_TARGET — also build time. Next.js evaluates rewrites() during the build and writes
# the result into routes-manifest.json, so setting it at `npm start` does nothing at all. It
# defaults to http://localhost:6011. Override it on the BUILD if the API is elsewhere:
#
#   API_PROXY_TARGET=http://10.0.0.5:6011 NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL= npm run build
#
# Only that private hop is ever compiled in, never the public address — which is the whole
# reason one build survives moving to a domain.

# The port this serves on, and the address it binds to.
#
# HOSTNAME=0.0.0.0 is required for anything outside the machine to reach it. The standalone
# server binds to localhost without it, which looks like a firewall problem and is not one.
PORT="6010"
HOSTNAME="0.0.0.0"

NODE_ENV="production"

# Behind nginx, this is the only port that needs to be open — and the API can stay on
# loopback entirely. See deploy/nginx/mtms.conf.
